C = [
("h1", "1. The Question and the Method"),
("p", "Claim: the question of the sign gifts is first a question about a word. Scripture has a vocabulary for what God does to mark a messenger or to move a people, and the vocabulary is used the same way across the Old Testament, the Gospels, Acts and the letters. The volume reads the vocabulary before it reads any claim about what continues or ceases, and it lets the passages say what a sign was for."),
("sub", "1.1 The three words together"),
("p", "The best-known list of the words stands in Peter’s sermon at Pentecost: “Jesus of Nazareth, a man approved of God among you by miracles and wonders and signs, which God did by him in the midst of you, as ye yourselves also know” (Acts 2:22). Three words are used, and the speaker joins them with “and.” The writer of Hebrews uses the same group when he writes of those who heard the Lord, “God also bearing them witness, both with signs and wonders, and with divers miracles, and gifts of the Holy Ghost, according to his own will” (Hebrews 2:4). Paul uses a different group in a different place: “the signs of an apostle were wrought among you in all patience, in signs, and wonders, and mighty deeds” (2 Corinthians 12:12)."),
("p", "The words are not interchangeable in every verse, and the volume does not treat them as one. The English “wonders” and “signs” translate two Greek words, and “miracles” and “mighty deeds” translate a third, the word for power. In Exodus the same English word “signs” stands in the Lord’s own speech: “I will harden Pharaoh’s heart, and multiply my signs and my wonders in the land of Egypt” (Exodus 7:3). The word names an act of God that points beyond itself. A wonder is what is seen; a sign is what the wonder is set to prove; a mighty deed is the power by which it is done. The passages above use the three together because a miracle of this kind is all three at once."),
("sub", "1.2 What ‘sign gifts’ names in this volume"),
("p", "The phrase ‘sign gifts’ does not appear in Scripture. The volume uses it for the powers the text itself calls signs, wonders, miracles or mighty deeds: healing, the casting out of devils, the raising of the dead, the speaking in tongues, and the other acts the passages place in that company. Paul links the matter to a gift in the letter to the Corinthians (“tongues are for a sign,” 1 Corinthians 14:22), and the writer to the Hebrews joins “gifts of the Holy Ghost” to the signs (Hebrews 2:4). The volume therefore uses the phrase for the signs as the passages describe them and for the gifts the passages join to them."),
("p", "The phrase does not carry a doctrine into the passages. It is a label for a set of verses, and the verses are read before the label is used. A reader who objects to the phrase may substitute ‘the signs, wonders and mighty deeds,’ and the argument stands."),
("sub", "1.3 Four questions put to every passage"),
("p", "The volume puts four questions to each passage that names a sign, in the same order every time. Who gave it? The passages answer: the LORD, the Lord Jesus, God by the hands of His servants. To whom was it given? The passages answer: Moses, Pharaoh, the elders of Israel, Hezekiah, the men of Israel, the Gentiles in Cyprus and in Lystra. What does the text say it was for? The passages answer in their own words: “that they may believe” (Exodus 4:5), “that ye may know how that I am the LORD” (Exodus 10:2), “confirming the word with signs following” (Mark 16:20). And what happened next? The passages answer with belief, with hardness, with a demand for another sign, or with silence."),
("p", "The fourth question matters as much as the other three. The record of signs is not a record of success. Israel sees the signs in Egypt and in the wilderness and is still charged: “How long will this people provoke me? and how long will it be ere they believe me, for all the signs which I have shewed among them?” (Numbers 14:11). The same pattern stands in the Gospels: “But though he had done so many miracles before them, yet they believed not on him” (John 12:37). A sign confirms; it does not compel."),
("sub", "1.4 What the volume reads and in what order"),
("p", "The volume reads the passages in the order the canon gives them. Chapter 2 reads the word “sign” where it names things that are not miracles. Chapters 3 and 4 read the signs of the Old Testament, first those that confirm a messenger and then those given to a nation or to a king. Chapter 5 reads the Lord’s signs in the Gospels, and chapter 6 the commission of the Twelve and the confirmation promised with it. Chapters 7 and 8 read the signs in Acts 1 to 8 and in Acts 9 to 28. Chapter 9 reads what Paul says of signs in his own letters. Chapter 10 reads the end of the record at Acts 28. Chapter 11 sets the position against the objections of modern writers and answers them."),
("p", "Some of these passages are argued elsewhere in the series and in other series, and the volume cites those places and does not repeat them. Where a passage has a textual question attached to it, the volume says so and leaves the question where it was argued."),
("ctbl", "What the method asks and what the passages answer.", [
    ("Question", "The passages answer", "Chief passages"),
    ("Who gave the sign?", "The LORD, the Lord Jesus, or God by the hands of His servants", "Exod 7:3; Acts 2:22; Heb 2:4"),
    ("To whom?", "Moses, Pharaoh, the elders and people of Israel, a king, the men of Israel, and in Acts the Gentiles who met Israel’s messengers", "Exod 4:30, 31; Isa 7:11; Acts 2:22"),
    ("For what?", "That they may believe; that they may know the LORD; that the word be confirmed", "Exod 4:5; Exod 10:2; Mark 16:20"),
    ("What followed?", "Belief in some; hardness and further demands in others", "Exod 4:31; Num 14:11; John 12:37"),
  ], [0.20, 0.50, 0.30]),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "Scripture joins signs, wonders and miracles in one group, and each word names a different side of the same act.",
    "A sign is an act of God given to a hearer for a stated purpose.",
    "The phrase ‘sign gifts’ is the volume’s label for a set of verses; it carries no doctrine into them.",
    "The record of signs is a record of confirmation, and it includes belief and refusal.",
    "Four questions are put to every passage: who gave it, to whom, for what, and what followed.",
  ]),
]
